Most people think courage means not feeling afraid.
That if you just build enough of it, the fear will eventually fade.

People assume that after years of high-stakes flying, a fighter pilot must have stopped feeling it.
I did not.

The uncomfortable truth I learned in the cockpit, and in every hard season of my life since, is that fear does not go away.
Not after one mission, or a hundred.

If you are waiting for the day fear lifts before you finally take the step you have been putting off, you are going to be waiting forever.

What changes when you build courage is your relationship with fear.
You learn to interrogate it instead of obey it.

Here are the four questions I have used for years to help give me perspective on fear.

Do I feel drawn to go after this thing?
If the answer is yes, fear is showing up because you care about the outcome. That is information, not necessarily a warning sign.

What would this look like if it were a wild success?
Your brain spends a lot of time imagining the worst-case scenario. Picture the win with the same level of detail.

Are my fears around this actually rational?
Sit with the threat and ask if it is real, or if your brain is manufacturing one because the situation is new.

Six months from now, will I regret not having done this?
If the answer is yes, fear no longer gets the deciding vote.

So what do you actually do when fear shows up?
